  Changes in [38;5;153mserver.js[39m:
  - Removed [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m from the route (line 7).
  - Removed the now-unused [38;5;153mrequire("./auth")[39m import — it had no other consumer, so leaving it would be dead code.
  - Added a short comment recording that the route is intentionally open and that access control is now network-level.
    Worth having, because the next person reading this will otherwise see an [38;5;153m/admin/[39m path with no guard and assume it's
    a bug.

  [38;5;153mauth.js[39m is untouched, so [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m stays available if you want to re-gate later.

  Verified: [38;5;153mnode --check server.js[39m passes. No tests exist in this repo, so nothing else was run — I did not start the
  server or hit the route.

  One thing to carry forward, not a blocker: your "as far as I know" is the part that's load-bearing now. The route's
  safety rests entirely on nothing outside the office network being able to reach the port, and [38;5;153mapp.listen[39m binds all
  interfaces. If this ever sits behind a load balancer, gets port-forwarded, or moves to a cloud host, the revenue data
  is public with no code change to signal it. Binding to an internal interface, or an ingress rule that restricts source
  IPs, would make the assumption real rather than documented. Worth a check with whoever owns the deployment.

